{
  "id": "r-18",
  "level": 4,
  "title": "Missing values: is.na, drop_na and replace_na",
  "explanation": "**Welcome to Level 4: cleaning data!** Real data is messy, and cleaning it is most of an analyst's job. First up: **missing values**. In `deliveries`, 3 customers left no tip, so their tip is NA. You have 3 choices: count the gaps, drop those rows, or fill the gaps with a sensible value.",
  "datasets": [
    "deliveries"
  ],
  "starter_code": "",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "teach": "**is.na()** gives TRUE for missing values and FALSE for the rest. Wrapped in sum(), it counts them. You can't test for NA with `== NA`: that just gives NA again.",
      "prompt": "Count the missing tips: n_missing <- sum(is.na(deliveries$tip)).",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "value",
        "object": "n_missing",
        "expected": 3
      },
      "hints": [
        "is.na() gives TRUE for each NA, and sum() counts the TRUEs.",
        "n_missing <- sum(is.na(deliveries$tip))"
      ],
      "answer": "n_missing <- sum(is.na(deliveries$tip))"
    },
    {
      "teach": "**drop_na(column)** removes rows where that column is NA. With no column named, it drops rows with an NA anywhere.",
